            <ArticleTitle>Pain management after Hurricane Katrina: outcomes of veterans enrolled in a New Orleans VA pain management program.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>440-6</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To evaluate Hurricane Katrina's impact on patients with pre-existing chronic pain. Design. Review of literature about Hurricane Katrina and chronic pain or pain management and a qualitative interview of all identified patients enrolled in a behavioral pain management program.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">Southeast Louisiana Veterans Healthcare System, Mental Health Service Line.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SUBJECTS" NlmCategory="METHODS">In total, 42 of the 53 (79%) veterans enrolled in a chronic pain program prior to Hurricane Katrina were able to be contacted and evaluated with interviews and a review of their available medical records.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OUTCOME MEASURES" NlmCategory="METHODS">Major impediments and facilitative factors in delivering pain management services were identified.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Health care planning for large-scale emergencies must take into the need for prompt continuation of pain management services in patients with chronic pre-existing pain. Coordination between emergency clinics and pain management specialists, as well as the availability of electronic medical records, is an important factor in continuing established pain management services after a regional disaster.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
